<!--page {"title": "Build-Ready Prototypes for Startups · Libre Studio", "description": "Clickable, build-ready prototypes for startups: real states and copy, on the tokens your developers will use. AI-native prototyping, decided by designers.", "path": "/prototyping/", "nav": "services"} -->
{{> head}}
{{> nav}}
<main id="main">

  <section class="section hero scheme-dark case-hero" aria-labelledby="proto-title">
    <div class="container hero__inner">
      <div class="hero__copy">
        <div class="stack">
          <p class="eyebrow" data-reveal>AI-native prototyping</p>
          <h1 class="h1" id="proto-title" data-split>Build-ready prototypes for <span class="mark">startups<svg class="mark__svg" viewBox="0 0 240 24" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" data-draw><path d="M4 15 C 46 8, 92 19, 136 12 S 210 7, 236 13"/></svg></span></h1>
          <p class="text-lg" data-reveal>Prototype before you build. We make clickable prototypes that behave like the product, so you can decide with something real in your hands, and your developers can carry it forward instead of starting again.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="actions actions--center" data-reveal>
          <a class="btn btn--lime" href="{{calendar}}" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span class="btn__label"><span data-text="Book a Call">Book a Call</span></span></a>
          <a class="btn btn--ghost-dark" href="/work/lula/"><span class="btn__label"><span data-text="See an example">See an example</span></span></a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section scheme-light" aria-labelledby="what-title">
    <div class="container split-rows">
      <div class="ai-def">
        <h2 class="h2" id="what-title" data-split>What is a build-ready prototype?</h2>
        <div class="text-lg prose" data-reveal>
          <p>A build-ready prototype is a clickable version of a product that uses real states, real copy and the same design tokens and components the developers will build with. Because it is made from the product's own system, it hands over to development instead of being thrown away.</p>
          <p>A throwaway prototype answers one question and then gets rebuilt from scratch. A build-ready one answers the question and becomes the reference the product is built from.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="ai-def">
        <h2 class="h2" id="why-title" data-split>Why prototype before you build?</h2>
        <div class="text-lg prose" data-reveal>
          <p>Code is the most expensive place to change your mind. A prototype lets you click through a flow, compare directions and settle the hard questions (what comes first, where the paywall sits, what a screen says) while a change still costs hours, not sprints.</p>
          <p>It also gives everyone the same thing to react to. Founders, investors and developers stop debating a description and start pointing at a screen.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section scheme-coral" aria-labelledby="kinds-title">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-title center narrow">
        <h2 class="h2" id="kinds-title" data-split>Which kind of prototype do you need?</h2>
        <p class="text-lg" data-reveal>We make three, and pick the lightest one that answers your question.</p>
      </div>
      <ol class="cycle" data-reveal-group>
        <li class="cycle__step" data-reveal>
          <span class="cycle__num">01</span>
          <h3 class="cycle__title">Fast explorations</h3>
          <p>Quick drafts in Figma Make or Claude Design to put several directions side by side.</p>
          <p><strong>For</strong> choosing a direction early.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="cycle__step" data-reveal>
          <span class="cycle__num">02</span>
          <h3 class="cycle__title">Clickable prototypes</h3>
          <p>HTML or React prototypes that behave like the product, not linked frames, built on your tokens and components.</p>
          <p><strong>For</strong> testing decisions on real interactions.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="cycle__step" data-reveal>
          <span class="cycle__num">03</span>
          <h3 class="cycle__title">Production front ends</h3>
          <p>Next.js and Tailwind CSS, with motion in GSAP or CSS, when the prototype should become the product.</p>
          <p><strong>For</strong> shipping what you tested.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section scheme-dark" aria-labelledby="ai-title">
    <div class="container ai-def">
      <h2 class="h2" id="ai-title" data-split>How does AI help with prototyping?</h2>
      <div class="text-lg prose" data-reveal>
        <p>AI lets us explore wide and fast, so many directions can be explored in days. Claude Code builds from the written specs and tokens, and stops to ask when no token fits.</p>
        <p>The choice is never the AI's. A senior designer decides which direction goes forward, and why, and approves every screen before you see it. The result is fewer, better decisions, not more screens. More on that in <a class="text-link" href="/ai-design/">how we design with AI</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section scheme-light" aria-labelledby="get-title">
    <div class="container split-rows">
      <div class="ai-def">
        <h2 class="h2" id="get-title" data-split>What you get</h2>
        <div class="text-lg prose" data-reveal>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>A clickable prototype</strong> with real copy and every state that matters: empty, loading, error, success.</li>
            <li><strong>Tokens and components</strong> named the same in Figma and in code, from our <a class="text-link" href="/design-systems/">atomic design system</a> work.</li>
            <li><strong>Written flow specs</strong> your developers and their AI tools can read.</li>
            <li><strong>A decision log</strong> of what we compared, what we chose and why.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>Not sure what to prototype yet? Start with <a class="text-link" href="/product-strategy/">product strategy</a>, or see the whole path from <a class="text-link" href="/idea-to-build-ready/">idea to build-ready product</a>. When the prototype should ship, that is <a class="text-link" href="/design-engineering/">design engineering</a>.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="ai-def">
        <h2 class="h2" id="lula-title" data-split>Lula: three onboarding prototypes</h2>
        <div class="text-lg prose" data-reveal>
          <p>For Lula, an AI bedtime-story app, we made three clickable onboarding prototypes in about two weeks. After an internal review of version one, we reworked eleven points, which led to the read-along paywall in version three: the first story comes before the paywall. The version-three onboarding is what the client's developers built. <a class="text-link" href="/work/lula/">Read the Lula case study</a>.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="phones" data-reveal-group>
        <figure class="phone" data-reveal><img src="/assets/img/work/lula/01_0a-720.webp" srcset="/assets/img/work/lula/01_0a-360.webp 360w, /assets/img/work/lula/01_0a-720.webp 720w" sizes="(min-width: 992px) 300px, 45vw" width="360" height="780" alt="Lula welcome screen: the star mascot Luna in a nightcap says hi." loading="lazy"><figcaption>Luna opens the onboarding.</figcaption></figure>
        <figure class="phone" data-reveal><img src="/assets/img/work/lula/08_2a-720.webp" srcset="/assets/img/work/lula/08_2a-360.webp 360w, /assets/img/work/lula/08_2a-720.webp 720w" sizes="(min-width: 992px) 300px, 45vw" width="360" height="780" alt="Lula onboarding question with illustrated cards for kindness and curiosity." loading="lazy"><figcaption>Every answer feeds the first story.</figcaption></figure>
        <figure class="phone" data-reveal><img src="/assets/img/work/lula/20_s11-720.webp" srcset="/assets/img/work/lula/20_s11-360.webp 360w, /assets/img/work/lula/20_s11-720.webp 720w" sizes="(min-width: 992px) 300px, 45vw" width="360" height="780" alt="Lula read-along screen ending with a Hear how it ends button that leads to the paywall." loading="lazy"><figcaption>Version three: the paywall arrives mid-story.</figcaption></figure>
      </div>
      <div class="ai-def">
        <h2 class="h2" id="tales-title" data-split>Tales AI: designed and built</h2>
        <div class="text-lg prose" data-reveal>
          <p>The pre-launch waitlist page for the same product went straight from design to code. We designed it and built it ourselves in Next.js, Tailwind CSS and GSAP, so nothing got lost between the two. <a class="text-link" href="/work/tales-ai-landing/">Read the Tales AI case study</a>.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <figure class="shot" data-reveal>
        <img src="/assets/img/work/tales/hero-1920.webp" srcset="/assets/img/work/tales/hero-960.webp 960w, /assets/img/work/tales/hero-1920.webp 1920w" sizes="(min-width: 1440px) 1280px, 92vw" width="1920" height="1200" alt="Tales AI landing page hero: the headline Your voice, Their adventure, an email field with a Join the Waitlist button, and storybook illustrations." loading="lazy">
        <figcaption>Tales AI landing page, built in Next.js.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section scheme-royal" aria-labelledby="proto-faq-title">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-title narrow">
        <h2 class="h2" id="proto-faq-title" data-split>FAQs</h2>
        <p class="text-lg" data-reveal>What founders ask before we prototype.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="faq__list" data-reveal>
        <details class="faq__item" data-accordion open>
          <summary class="faq__q"><span>Figma prototype or coded prototype?</span><img class="faq__icon" src="/assets/img/figma/chevron-up.svg" width="32" height="32" alt=""></summary>
          <div class="faq__a" data-accordion-body><div><p>Figma Make or Claude Design when you need to compare directions quickly. A coded prototype in HTML or React when a decision depends on how the product behaves: timing, input, transitions, real content. Coded prototypes also hand over more directly to developers.</p></div></div>
        </details>
        <details class="faq__item" data-accordion>
          <summary class="faq__q"><span>Can we test it with users?</span><img class="faq__icon" src="/assets/img/figma/chevron-up.svg" width="32" height="32" alt=""></summary>
          <div class="faq__a" data-accordion-body><div><p>Yes. A clickable prototype is designed to be put in front of people, so you can see how they move through a flow before anything is built. We shape the prototype around the questions you want answered.</p></div></div>
        </details>
        <details class="faq__item" data-accordion>
          <summary class="faq__q"><span>Does the prototype become the product?</span><img class="faq__icon" src="/assets/img/figma/chevron-up.svg" width="32" height="32" alt=""></summary>
          <div class="faq__a" data-accordion-body><div><p>It can. Because it uses the same tokens and components as the build, your developers can carry it forward. When the prototype should become the product, we build the front end in Next.js and Tailwind CSS ourselves.</p></div></div>
        </details>
        <details class="faq__item" data-accordion>
          <summary class="faq__q"><span>How long does it take?</span><img class="faq__icon" src="/assets/img/figma/chevron-up.svg" width="32" height="32" alt=""></summary>
          <div class="faq__a" data-accordion-body><div><p>It depends on the scope. For Lula, three clickable onboarding prototypes took about two weeks. We agree the scope with you on a first call.</p></div></div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {{> work-next}}
</main>
{{> footer}}
